ecides  that the child of the banker would have  been likely to earn more in the future  that to me goes right against the  founding principle of equality that  underlines the way the nhs works and we  should change that as well  um i wonder mr hull if we can kind of  stay on your brief such as it is but get  you get your your kind of experience as  a former health sector and of course you  know when i'm going with this the high  court decision the high court uh  judicial review yesterday of matt  hancock's decision to move uh people  elderly people from hospitals uh into  care homes  the prime minister said yesterday at  pmqs that the government wasn't told of  concerns about asymptomatic transmission  by public health england and that's  nonsense isn't it  well i think the first thing to say is  that the you know to be prime minister  or health secretary in a pandemic is an  incredibly challenging thing you're  having to make decisions very quickly  and you are going to make some mistakes  and i think we have to be reasonable and  think about the context that matt  hancock and boris johnson were operating  in  our select committee looked at this  report in detail and we concluded  similarly to yesterday's judgment that  we should have done much more to learn  from what happened in countries like  germany and hong kong  where when people were sent to care  homes for example in germany they had to  be isolated for three months unless  there was a negative test uh we didn't  do that here that was clearly a mistake  but i think the broader issue and it's  one that i have to bear my share of  responsibility for as well as someone  who has health secretary for six years  is that we just still have not sorted  out problems in the social care system  so when you have a crisis  it always ends up playing second fiddle  to the nhs the focus of if i may say  you'll also the media but the  politicians the civil servants the  system is on hospitals and hospital care  and that's what everyone is thinking  about  and  what happened  i would counter that we probably  wouldn't have known as quickly as we did  what was going on in our care homes if  it wasn't for the media including people  like our people in politics  correspondent nick martin getting in  there i mean the problem is i think i  suppose mr hunt is we can't deal with  this as an abstraction you know by june  of 2020 there were perhaps as many as 30  000 excess deaths and care homes and in  part that was as a result of a decision  taken by the health the then health  secretary seemingly unaware of advice  that if he'd switched on the today  program he'd have heard sir patrick  violence giving  yes and so i wasn't saying that the  media didn't have a very very important  role during the i was being somewhat  cheeky i was being somewhat cheap  but i think the the interesting thing is  actually if you look at the high court  judgment the advice the health secretary  received from officials  didn't advise him that he should think  about this question of asymptomatic  transmission so there was a kind of  group think in the system that said that  this uh virus we were dealing with was  going to be essentially like the flu  virus where you don't think so much  about asymptomatic transmission i think  there are lots and lots of lessons to be  learned but the broader point i really  wanted to make is that until we really  fix the problems in the care system  then i think we're always going to find  the center of gravity in a crisis  remains hospitals and the nhs  and that in this case had devastating  consequences  mr hunt away from away from health  entirely and what do you make of the  reports that the one of your  parliamentary colleagues uh one of your  conservative party colleagues i should  actually say um was seen by his  colleagues watching pornography on his  mobile phone in the house of commons  chamber  well to be honest i just feel so  disappointed um i'm disappointed that it 